  "configuration_id": "config_026",
  "reason": "With no prior results, the key uncertainty is whether a multimodal (RNA+image) single-profile model with a biologically-informed RNA encoder and a standard CNN image encoder provides a strong baseline. config_026 (multimodal, singleprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, weighted fusion) tests this core premise: it uses scvi_n200 (a learned encoder capturing cell-type structure, likely superior to PCA for downstream classification) paired with resnet50 (a solid but not maximally expensive image backbone) and a simple weighted fusion. This gives a competitive reference point at moderate cost—scvi_n200 is more expensive than PCA but far cheaper than scgpt, resnet50 is cheaper than ViT-L, and singleprofile avoids the overhead of multiprofile aggregation. Establishing this baseline early lets subsequent experiments efficiently probe whether upgrading the image encoder (ViT-L), switching to multiprofile, or changing fusion/aggregation yields meaningful gains, rather than wasting budget on an overly expensive first trial. Expected relative cost: moderate (roughly 1.5–2× a PCA+resnet50 singleprofile run, well below multiprofile+ViT-L+transformer configurations)."
}

